WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.401
Build security determines what enters the container supply chain.

2
00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:18.375
Dockerfiles and similar build definitions should be reviewed for base image choices, package installation patterns, user configuration, copied files, exposed ports, entry points, and build-time assumptions.

3
00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:32.427
Build context control matters because accidental inclusion of source trees, credentials, configuration files, or temporary artifacts can place sensitive material inside an image or expose it to the build system.

4
00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:37.599
Reproducible build concepts help teams understand and trust their artifacts.

5
00:00:37.599 --> 00:00:47.327
Dependency pinning, controlled package sources, deterministic build steps, and clear artifact naming make it easier to investigate what changed.

6
00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:58.522
Multi-stage builds separate build-time dependencies from smaller runtime artifacts, reducing the amount of tooling and intermediate content that ships to production.

7
00:00:58.522 --> 00:01:04.776
The runtime image should contain what is needed to run, not everything needed to compile or test.

8
00:01:04.776 --> 00:01:07.864
Secrets during build are a common failure path.

9
00:01:07.864 --> 00:01:20.989
Credentials used to fetch private dependencies, publish artifacts, or access registries should not be baked into image layers, printed in logs, or stored in build arguments that become visible later.

10
00:01:20.989 --> 00:01:28.864
CI/CD systems should provide secure secret injection patterns and limit who can read or modify pipeline configuration.

11
00:01:28.864 --> 00:01:32.416
The build system is part of the production trust chain.

12
00:01:32.416 --> 00:01:43.456
Pipeline protections include branch protections, required review, artifact integrity checks, automated scanning, registry publishing controls, and policy gates.

13
00:01:43.456 --> 00:01:46.313
The point is not to make every build painful.

14
00:01:46.313 --> 00:01:55.346
The point is to prevent untrusted code, leaked secrets, unreviewed artifacts, or known risky images from moving quietly into production.

15
00:01:55.346 --> 00:01:59.901
Build security gives runtime security a trustworthy starting point.
